x751 text "A file starts with zero or more `use \"path.a0\"` lines, relative to the file. The linker loads every used file once, orders them by dependency, and validates the result as one program with one namespace. Cycles are rejected. A function name defined in two files is rejected with both locations. Diagnostics on a linked program name the file and line they belong to."
x752 puts x750 x751
x753 call close x752 
x754 call open x753 10
x755 write x754 4
x756 write x755 2
x757 text "code"
x758 puts x756 x757
x759 call open x758 4
x760 write x759 2
x761 text "use \"ui.a0\"\nuse \"../examples/life.a0\"\nfn page io -> io\na call open p0 5\nb call close a\nret b\nend"
x762 puts x760 x761
x763 call close x762 
x764 call close x763 
x765 call close x764 
x766 call open x765 13
x767 write x766 4
x768 write x767 1
x769 text "editing"
x770 puts x768 x769
x771 call open x770 11
x772 write x771 2
x773 text "Views and edits"
x774 puts x772 x773
x775 call close x774 
x776 call open x775 2
x777 write x776 2
x778 text "A model never sees a whole file. It asks for a view of one function: the function with a handle line on top (e0), one signature line per callee, and a program handle (g0). The program view is scoped: a comment line with the function count, then the signatures of the target, its transitive callees, and its direct callers, so it stays the same size as the program grows to thousands of functions. A reply is the handle line followed by edits, one per line: `id op ...` replaces the instruction id or inserts it before ret; `id op ... @ other` inserts after other; `-id` deletes; `ret x` changes the result. Under g0, a whole `fn ... end` block adds or replaces a function and `-fn name` removes one. The edit is applied only if it parses, type-checks, validates, and was written against the current revision; otherwise it is rejected and the handle stays valid."
x779 puts x777 x778
x780 call close x779 
x781 call open x780 10
x782 write x781 4
x783 write x782 2
x784 text "code"
x785 puts x783 x784
x786 call open x785 4
x787 call open x786 6
x788 write x787 4
x789 write x788 2
x790 text "cm"
x791 puts x789 x790
x792 write x791 2
x793 text "# a view of clamp, then a reply that fixes the upper bound\n"
x794 puts x792 x793
x795 call close x794 
x796 write x795 2
x797 text "e0 fn clamp u32 u32 u32 -> u32\nlo lt p0 p1\nhi lt p2 p0\na select lo p1 p0\nr select hi p2 a\nret r\n\n"
x798 puts x796 x797
x799 call open x798 6
x800 write x799 4
x801 write x800 2
x802 text "cm"
x803 puts x801 x802
x804 write x803 2
x805 text "# reply\n"
x806 puts x804 x805
x807 call close x806 
x808 write x807 2
x809 text "e0\nhi lt p2 a\nr select hi p2 a"
x810 puts x808 x809
x811 call close x810 
x812 call close x811 
x813 call close x812 
x814 call open x813 13
x815 write x814 4
x816 write x815 1
x817 text "diagnostics"
x818 puts x816 x817
x819 call open x818 11
x820 write x819 2
x821 text "Diagnostics"
x822 puts x820 x821
x823 call close x822 
x824 call open x823 2
x825 write x824 2
x826 text "Every rejection carries a stable code (parse, type, structure, handle, revision, limit), what was expected, what was seen, and the one fix that resolves it. The compiler never guesses: a reply that is ambiguous is rejected with the fix, not applied approximately."
x827 puts x825 x826
x828 call close x827 
x829 call close x828 
ret x829
end

fn sec_run io -> io
x831 call open p0 13
x832 write x831 4
x833 write x832 1
x834 text "bounds"
x835 puts x833 x834
x836 call open x835 11
x837 write x836 2
x838 text "Bounds and safety"
x839 puts x837 x838
x840 call close x839 
x841 call open x840 2
x842 write x841 2
x843 text "Static caps: 65536 functions per program (also after linking), 4096 instructions per function, 64 parameters, arrays up to 65536 elements (1024 on the hardware and GPU targets), literal iteration budget 2^24 per function. Variable iteration counts are bounded by fuel in the reference interpreter; compiled targets have no execution budget. io output is bounded by the caller's buffer. There is no heap, no recursion, no exceptions, and no undefined behavior: div and rem by zero are defined, shifts mask the count, indices wrap modulo the array length."
x844 puts x842 x843
x845 call close x844 
x846 call close x845 
x847 call open x846 13
x848 write x847 4
x849 write x848 1
x850 text "cli"
